\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {End-to-end Python sensing, representation-learning and adaptation pipeline.} The two source archives are asymmetric: Room~A arrives as raw Nexmon \texttt  {.pcap} and must be decoded to amplitude in Python, while Room~B is already amplitude \texttt  {.csv}. Both are brought into one 256-column intermediate form, after which the pipeline is source-agnostic. The original capture is the atomic unit of the development/validation split, so every window derived from a capture stays on one side of it. Windows are stored un-normalised and a preset is applied at load time, which lets all three tasks share a single \texttt  {windows/} directory. Both rooms use the same radio platform throughout: 5\,GHz, 80\,MHz, BCM4366c0 with Nexmon CSI extraction.}}{3}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:pipeline_diagram}{{1}{3}{\textbf {End-to-end Python sensing, representation-learning and adaptation pipeline.} The two source archives are asymmetric: Room~A arrives as raw Nexmon \texttt {.pcap} and must be decoded to amplitude in Python, while Room~B is already amplitude \texttt {.csv}. Both are brought into one 256-column intermediate form, after which the pipeline is source-agnostic. The original capture is the atomic unit of the development/validation split, so every window derived from a capture stays on one side of it. Windows are stored un-normalised and a preset is applied at load time, which lets all three tasks share a single \texttt {windows/} directory. Both rooms use the same radio platform throughout: 5\,GHz, 80\,MHz, BCM4366c0 with Nexmon CSI extraction}{figure.caption.1}{}}
